<DocsLayout
  title="Sidecar"
  subtitle="The local Bun service that owns persistence, authenticated HTTP, manufacturing services, project history, and the assistant."
  description="CADCAMFUN sidecar: startup protocol, loopback API, configuration, service wiring, shutdown, observability, and companion gateway."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Runtime responsibilities</h2>
<p>The sidecar is the Bun backend shared by the desktop and local development integrations. It opens SQLite, supplies repositories and domain services, serves the schema-defined HTTP API, and hosts assistant and workflow integration. The Tauri shell starts the packaged binary and discovers its assigned port from a machine-readable startup event.</p>
    <p><code>apps/sidecar/src/layers.ts</code> composes the served API, handlers, authorization, repositories, and services. Optional providers such as paid checkout and AI models are configured through their services; their absence does not turn a database record into a working external integration.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="process" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Process and shutdown protocol</h2>
<p>The main HTTP server binds only <code>127.0.0.1</code>, with port <code>0</code> so the operating system chooses an available port. HTTP idle timeout is disabled to allow a long assistant call to remain silent between streamed events. Graceful HTTP shutdown is configured for 500 milliseconds before scope finalization continues.</p>
    <p>After the API's public health probe succeeds, the process prints a ready event on stdout. Startup failure prints an error event and sets a failing exit code. Operational logs go to stderr as JSON; stdout is reserved for the parent process protocol.</p>
    <DocCode language="text" title="Startup protocol examples">
{`{"event":"ready","port":53211}
{"event":"error","message":"Startup failure details"}`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>A newline-terminated stdin message with <code>event: "shutdown"</code>, stdin closure, SIGINT, or SIGTERM releases the server scope. Malformed parent messages are ignored, and an oversized incomplete line is discarded. Finalizers stop owned services and checkpoint/close SQLite. Interrupt-only termination is reported as a clean exit.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="config" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Required configuration</h2>
<p><code>AppConfigService</code> reads process configuration once in <code>apps/sidecar/src/config.ts</code>. <code>CADCAMFUN_TOKEN</code> is the required application bearer token and <code>CADCAMFUN_DATA_DIR</code> is the required application data directory. The database path is <code>&lt;dataDir&gt;/cadcamfun.db</code>. <code>CADCAMFUN_MIGRATIONS_DIR</code> is optional and supplies the SQL migration folder; without it the database layer does not apply application migrations.</p>
    <p>The desktop supplies launch configuration. For direct development, provide those configuration names in the launching shell and run the package's start script from the repository root:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Start a configured sidecar">
{`bun run --filter @cadcamfun/sidecar start`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>The sidecar package defines <code>start</code> and <code>typecheck</code> scripts. Packaged binary compilation is owned by the repository release scripts, rather than a sidecar-package <code>build</code> command.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="environment" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Optional configuration</h2>
<ul>
      <li><code>CADCAMFUN_APP_URL</code> sets the base for authentication links; <code>CADCAMFUN_DEBUG_LINKS</code> enables returning debug reset/magic links and defaults to false.</li>
      <li><code>CADCAMFUN_AI_HOST</code> is <code>nikcli</code> (default; chat runs through the embedded conversation host) or <code>direct</code> (AI SDK only). <code>CADCAMFUN_AI_OFFLINE</code> (boolean, default false) disables the optional model-catalogue network path. <code>CADCAMFUN_AI_MINIMAX_BASE_URL</code> overrides MiniMax's endpoint.</li>
      <li><code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code> enables the Stripe provider; <code>STRIPE_PRICE_BASIC</code>, <code>STRIPE_PRICE_PRO</code>, and <code>STRIPE_PRICE_ENTERPRISE</code> supply paid-plan prices. <code>STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET</code> is required for signed webhook verification.</li>
      <li><code>CRON_SECRET</code> supplies the cron route secret; without it the local application can call that route without an additional cron secret. <code>ADMIN_EMAIL</code> is a legacy administrator identity check; by default the local profile is the administrator of its own installation.</li>
      <li><code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code> enables the configured OTLP telemetry export. Without an endpoint, external telemetry export is off.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>All of these names are read in one place, <code>AppConfigService</code>; the token and Stripe secret key are held as redacted values so they do not appear in logs. AI provider keys are managed through the credential API and dedicated credential file. They are not read as vendor API-key environment settings by <code>AppConfigService</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="served" class="doc-section">
    <h2>HTTP access and API discovery</h2>
<p>The sidecar publishes <code>/api/openapi.json</code> for its served contract and uses bearer middleware on protected groups. A second optional session header chooses the acting account. The local profile is created or reused under <code>local@cadcamfun.local</code>, keeping the installation usable without account sign-in.</p>
    <p>CORS admits <code>tauri://localhost</code>, HTTP/HTTPS <code>tauri.localhost</code>, and HTTP localhost or 127.0.0.1 origins with an optional port. Other origins receive no allowed-origin response header. CORS is a browser policy; the bearer middleware remains the access control for protected routes.</p>
    <p>The served groups cover design resources, manufacturing generation and verification, exact CAD, libraries, costs, AI, VCS/workflows, events, companion controls, identity, organizations, notifications, files, plugins, billing, analytics, waitlist, MCP, and local state. Consult the running OpenAPI document for method and payload details.</p>
    <p>The acting-account header is <code>x-session-token</code>. Without it, or with a token that does not verify as a session token for an existing user, requests act as the local profile (<code>src/http/authorization.ts</code>).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="layout" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Source organization</h2>
<p><code>main.ts</code> launches the scoped server and parent protocol. <code>config.ts</code> supplies configuration, <code>layers.ts</code> wires dependencies, <code>startup.ts</code> shares readiness and the loopback port, <code>shutdown.ts</code> reads parent shutdown messages, and <code>observability.ts</code> configures optional telemetry.</p>
    <p><code>http/</code> maps validated requests to services. <code>repos/</code> maps resources to Drizzle queries and resource access rules. <code>services/</code> coordinates cross-resource behavior, and <code>plugins/</code> contains worker-host and browser bootstrap implementations.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="http-files" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Handler groups</h2>
<p>Handlers are grouped by resource: projects, drawings, components, toolpaths and extras, the toolpath generator, exact CAD, materials/tools/machine configurations and their storage routes, library, costs, files, organizations, notifications, user/auth, billing, analytics, waitlist, MCP, miscellaneous application services, key-value state, AI, VCS, events, and companion management.</p>
    <p><code>entity-io.ts</code> shares entity import/export helpers. <code>authorization.ts</code> applies application and scoped bridge credentials. A handler's declared payload has already passed schema decoding; service-level validation still checks references, ownership, evidence consistency, and domain constraints.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="services" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Domain service composition</h2>
<p>AI services supply drawing and CAM actions, parametric/exact inspection, project context, memory, worker journals, and plugin tools. <code>cam-program.ts</code> reruns captured manufacturing requests and validates native evidence during saves. History services apply snapshots, protected-branch rules, GitHub transport, and workflow execution.</p>
    <p>Other services own file storage, cost calculations, local profile and acting-user state, token signing/revocation, bridge credentials, payments, notifications, live events, and companion lifecycle. Effect layers own their resources so shutdown releases them in dependency order.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="repos" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Repository boundary</h2>
<p>Repositories use the shared <code>Database.query</code> service for persistence and typed storage failures. They decode stored JSON and enforce resource-specific access or provide authorization helpers. HTTP handlers translate domain/storage failures into their declared response errors; geometry calculations stay in <code>@cadcamfun/core</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="plugin-host" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Plugin integration</h2>
<p>The general worker host is in <code>plugins/host.ts</code>. The production assistant plugin service loads explicitly enabled <code>ai:tool</code> extensions from the application plugin directory. The browser ZIP installer and bootstrap API use their own manifest and storage paths. See <a href="/docs/plugins">Plugins</a> before selecting an extension format or assuming a capability is wired.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="companion-routes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Companion HTTPS gateway</h2>
<p>The companion gateway is a separate opt-in HTTPS listener that can resume a previously enabled setting. It binds IPv4 for network clients, presents a generated certificate retained under <code>&lt;dataDir&gt;/companion</code>, and uses scoped revocable device tokens. Pairing supplies a certificate fingerprint so the mobile transport can pin the desktop.</p>
    <p>The gateway checks an explicit route allow-list and forwards admitted requests to the loopback API with the application token. A paired phone never receives that application token. The allow-list excludes credential administration, plugin installation, file management, and billing. Revoking a device or disabling the gateway removes its network access.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ready" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Readiness and diagnostics</h2>
<p>The ready event means the local HTTP health probe succeeded after server layer construction. It does not mean a model provider has accepted a request, checkout is configured, or every optional network service is reachable. Diagnose those services through their own status and result endpoints.</p>
    <p>For a startup error, retain the protocol message and relevant stderr events, check configuration and migrations, and inspect the data directory's accessibility. Never paste the launch bearer token or provider credential contents into diagnostic output.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Data ownership</h2>
<p>Persistent data includes the SQLite database and migration backups, file storage, AI credential/configuration state, authentication secret and revocation records, plugin assets, and companion certificates. Protect the whole application data directory when backing it up or transferring an installation. The sidecar owns connection lifetime; clients own neither the SQLite handle nor the main bearer token's issuance.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="development-launch" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Launch a disposable local instance</h2>
<p>Run these commands at the repository root in a dedicated terminal. The directory is new, the token is generated locally without printing it, migrations are explicit, and direct/offline AI mode avoids importing the embedded conversation host for this startup check. <code>openssl</code> supplies the random launch token in this shell example.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Explicit development configuration">
{String.raw`export CADCAMFUN_DATA_DIR="$(mktemp -d /tmp/cadcamfun-docs.XXXXXX)"
export CADCAMFUN_TOKEN="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
export CADCAMFUN_MIGRATIONS_DIR="$PWD/packages/db/drizzle"
export CADCAMFUN_AI_HOST=direct
export CADCAMFUN_AI_OFFLINE=1
bun run --filter @cadcamfun/sidecar start`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Read the ready event's port rather than assuming a fixed number. Keep stdin open while the service runs; piping from an already-ended input source asks it to shut down. Use SIGTERM or the shutdown protocol to release the database. The disposable directory remains available for inspection after exit.</p>
    <p>With a connection URL and its token already available in a client shell, compare public liveness with protected access:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Probe a running instance">
{String.raw`curl --fail-with-body "$CADCAMFUN_URL/api/health"
curl --fail-with-body -H "Authorization: Bearer $CADCAMFUN_TOKEN" "$CADCAMFUN_URL/version"
curl --fail-with-body -H "Authorization: Bearer $CADCAMFUN_TOKEN" "$CADCAMFUN_URL/api/projects"`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Source: <code>apps/sidecar/src/main.ts</code>, <code>config.ts</code>, <code>shutdown.ts</code>, and <code>test/process.test.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="observability" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Logs and optional telemetry</h2>
<p>Stderr JSON logs and stdout ready/error events have different consumers. A launcher should parse complete stdout lines and forward or retain stderr separately. A request that fails after readiness is an application error, not a second startup event.</p>
    <p>When <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code> is configured, <code>ObservabilityLive</code> sets up batching for spans/logs and periodic metric export. It appends <code>/v1/traces</code>, <code>/v1/metrics</code>, and <code>/v1/logs</code> to the supplied collector base after removing trailing slashes. Supply a collector base such as <code>http://collector:4318</code>, rather than an already suffixed signal URL.</p>
    <p>The resource service name is <code>cadcamfun-sidecar</code> and its version comes from application configuration. Standard OpenTelemetry resource environment settings are handled by the SDK. Network export is optional, while local stderr remains available when no collector is configured.</p>
    <p>Source: <code>apps/sidecar/src/observability.ts</code> and <code>main.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="troubleshooting" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Identify the failing boundary</h2>
<table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely boundary and check</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Process exits before ready</td><td>Read the protocol error and stderr. Check required config, directory access, migration identity, and service construction.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Health answers but protected routes return 401</td><td>Use the token for this launch, not an account session token or a token from a prior launch. A bridge token opens only its two action routes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A request works in curl but fails in a browser</td><td>Inspect browser origin and preflight response. The allow-list permits the supported Tauri and HTTP loopback origins; bearer access is still required.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ready port is unreachable after launch</td><td>Check whether the parent closed stdin or the shell restarted the child. The main API binds loopback only and is not the phone-facing gateway.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Companion gateway fails to enable</td><td>Read certificate, port-bind, and persisted-settings errors. Explicit ports must be integers from 1024 to 65535; failure to save settings stops the new listener.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Data appears in one tool but not another</td><td>Compare explicit database paths for the sidecar, web Studio, and migration CLI before assuming resources were lost.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The real-process lifecycle tests cover EOF, a split shutdown JSON line, and SIGTERM, then restart the same data directory and verify persisted projects. They also inspect the WAL after exit. This establishes the source process's lifecycle behavior; packaged shell and machine-specific delivery still require their own validation.</p>
    <p>Source: <code>apps/sidecar/test/process.test.ts</code>, <code>src/http/authorization.ts</code>, <code>src/layers.ts</code>, and <code>src/services/companion.ts</code>.</p>
  </section>

    <section id="startup-sequence" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Startup sequence in detail</h2>
    <figure class="not-prose my-6">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 230" role="img" aria-label="Startup sequence between the Tauri shell, the sidecar and the webview" style="width:100%;max-width:680px;height:auto">
        <g font-family="monospace" font-size="12" fill="currentColor">
          <text x="40" y="18">Tauri shell</text><text x="290" y="18">sidecar (Bun)</text><text x="520" y="18">webview</text>
        </g>
        <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3"><line x1="80" y1="25" x2="80" y2="225"/><line x1="330" y1="25" x2="330" y2="225"/><line x1="560" y1="25" x2="560" y2="225"/></g>
        <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none">
          <path d="M80 45 H330"/><path d="M330 85 H80"/><path d="M560 125 H80"/><path d="M80 150 H560"/><path d="M560 185 H330"/><path d="M80 215 H330"/>
        </g>
        <g font-family="monospace" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">
          <text x="90" y="40">spawn: CADCAMFUN_TOKEN (64 hex), DATA_DIR, MIGRATIONS_DIR</text>
          <text x="140" y="80">stdout &#123;"event":"ready","port":N&#125; (≤ 60 s)</text>
          <text x="330" y="120">invoke get_sidecar_endpoint (waits ≤ 125 s)</text>
          <text x="330" y="145">&#123; url, token &#125;</text>
          <text x="370" y="180">GET /api/health, then API calls</text>
          <text x="90" y="210">quit: stdin &#123;"event":"shutdown"&#125;</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </figure>
    <ol>
      <li>The shell generates a launch token of 32 random bytes, hex-encoded as 64 characters, and starts the sidecar with <code>CADCAMFUN_TOKEN</code>, <code>CADCAMFUN_DATA_DIR</code>, and <code>CADCAMFUN_MIGRATIONS_DIR</code>.</li>
      <li><code>AppConfigService</code> reads all settings once. A missing required setting fails startup with an <code>error</code> event.</li>
      <li>The database layer opens <code>&lt;dataDir&gt;/cadcamfun.db</code>, enables WAL mode and foreign keys, and applies migrations from the migrations directory.</li>
      <li>Layers are composed: database, then repositories, then services, then HTTP handlers. Every resource is owned by a layer, so shutdown releases it in reverse dependency order.</li>
      <li>The HTTP server binds <code>127.0.0.1</code> on port 0, and the operating system picks a free port.</li>
      <li>The public health route is probed. Only after it answers is <code>&#123;"event":"ready","port":N&#125;</code> written to stdout.</li>
      <li>The shell waits up to 60 seconds for that line and restarts the sidecar at most once. The webview waits up to 125 seconds for <code>get_sidecar_endpoint</code>, then connects.</li>
    </ol>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stream</th><th>Carries</th><th>Consumer</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>stdout</td><td>Exactly one <code>ready</code> or <code>error</code> JSON line</td><td>The parent process (shell or your script).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>stderr</td><td>JSON operational logs</td><td>Log files and diagnostics. Lines that may carry credentials (for example containing <code>Bearer</code>, <code>secret</code>, or <code>cadcamfun_token</code>) are redacted by the shell.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>stdin</td><td><code>&#123;"event":"shutdown"&#125;</code> lines; EOF also shuts down</td><td>The sidecar's shutdown reader. Oversized or malformed lines are ignored.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="auth-model" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Authentication model</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Credential</th><th>Where it comes from</th><th>What it opens</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Launch token (<code>Authorization: Bearer</code>)</td><td>Generated by the shell per launch, or set by you for manual runs</td><td>Every protected route of the loopback API.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Session token (<code>x-session-token</code>)</td><td>Sign-in flows; signed by a per-installation secret stored next to the database</td><td>Selects the acting user. If missing or invalid, requests act as <code>local@cadcamfun.local</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bridge token</td><td><code>POST /api/ai/bridge/tokens</code>; shown once</td><td>Only the action listing and invocation routes, for one project, read or read-write, until expiry (default 30 days, maximum 365).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Companion device token</td><td>Pairing; shown once inside the pairing link; stored only as a SHA-256 hash</td><td>The companion gateway's allow-listed routes, over HTTPS.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Signed tokens use HS256 with issuer <code>cadcamfun</code>. Revoked tokens verify as <code>invalid</code>, and unknown or forged tokens are ignored by revocation. The signing secret is generated once per installation and stored in the data directory, so links issued before a restart still verify afterwards.</p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Keep the launch token out of logs">
      <p>Treat the launch token like a password for the whole local API. Scripts should read it from the environment and never print it. Use a bridge or device token when another program or device needs narrower access.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="data-directory" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What lives in the data directory</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>cadcamfun.db</code> (+ <code>-wal</code>, <code>-shm</code>)</td><td>SQLite database in WAL mode.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>auth-secret</code></td><td>Per-installation signing secret for session and link tokens.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>companion/</code></td><td>Gateway certificate and private key (owner-only permissions).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>plugins/</code></td><td><code>enabled.json</code> and one folder per assistant plugin.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>file storage</td><td>Uploaded files (local bucket).</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Back up the whole directory while the sidecar is stopped, or after a shutdown that checkpointed the WAL. Copying only <code>cadcamfun.db</code> while the sidecar runs can miss recent writes that are still in the WAL file.</p>
  </section>

    <section id="ready-line" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The ready line, captured</h2>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Start a disposable sidecar and read its port">
{"CADCAMFUN_TOKEN=t CADCAMFUN_DATA_DIR=\u0022$(mktemp -d)\u0022 \\\nCADCAMFUN_MIGRATIONS_DIR=\u0022$PWD/packages/db/drizzle\u0022 \\\nCADCAMFUN_AI_HOST=direct CADCAMFUN_AI_OFFLINE=true \\\nbun --no-env-file apps/sidecar/src/main.ts\n# stdout: {\u0022event\u0022:\u0022ready\u0022,\u0022port\u0022:43461}"}
    </DocCode>
    <p>The port is chosen by the operating system on every start. The line is the first stdout output after migrations and layers are ready, so a client that sees it can call the API immediately. Keep stdin open: closing it is the shutdown signal.</p>
  </section>

